  "review": {
    "review": "The hero died while starving and under observed vampire-bat pressure; the recording does not identify a definitive killer. Earlier fighting reduced HP from 26 to 5 (decisions 5386–5393). A separate initiating defect prolonged the terminal chase: decisions 5405, 5408 and 5411 selected doorway scouting despite a fast bat following. At decision 5408 (log 16180), the hero had 4/85 HP, a charged known missile staff, and a bat one cell behind; the proposed step left a two-cell gap. The recorded species report already included fast movement, but the exposure scorer checked only speed buffs or particular description wording. This incorrectly bypassed existing low-health and water-scout exposure checks. The accepted move at log 16181 consumed a turn and reduced HP to 3; starvation prevents attributing that loss to an attack. The final targeting receipt at log 16199 confirms HP 3 to 0. The proposed correction consistently includes existing fast-species capabilities in pursuit exposure. A reduced public-observation fixture reproduces the original scout and, after correction, selects an existing charged stationary attack. This fixture includes explicitly reconstructed inspection bookkeeping, not recovered original runtime memory. The new six-test regression has four expected assertion failures against original source and passes on the candidate; the full suite passes 2,279 tests with four skips. Neither a guaranteed kill nor improved survival is established.",
    "learnings": "Use the existing fast-movement species capability consistently when evaluating short pursuit gaps; an empty buff list does not make a vampire bat slow. Keep pursuit risk separate from current melee contact, and verify actual damage, defeat and pickup receipts. The isolated fixture demonstrates a different admitted selection, not encounter success."
  },
